Here is the Python script that solves your problem. This function iterates over a list of numbers and checks if each number in turn satisfies an inequality condition (the current element should be greater than or equal to its previous one). If it finds such index, then returns this position as result; otherwise -1 will return at last iteration:
```python 
def can_arrange(arr):  
    for i in range(len(arr)-2,-1,-1): # iterate over the list from end to start (to check if current element is greater than or equal)    
        if arr[i] >= arr[i+1]: 
            return i + 1;         # Return index of first number that violates condition, which will be returned by function.  
    return -1                    # If no such elements found in the list then returns '-1'. This is equivalent to returning None from a Python context where you can't directly get an integer as output (like when calling this method inside another program).  It means that there are not any numbers greater than or equal for arr[i] and it will return -1.
```  
This function works in O(n) time complexity, which is the best possible solution because we have to check every element at least once (in worst case scenario when all elements of array would be increasing). The space complexity also depends on input size but as this problem does not require any extra memory then it can always run with constant amount.

